{"name":"Finn","hex":"#694554","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Finn","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A national of Finland.","A person of ethnic Finnish (Suomi) ancestry; a non-Swedish, non-Sami Finlander."]},{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A male given name from Old Norse."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Finn"}],"entries":[{"title":"Finnic peoples","blocks":[{"text":"The Finnic peoples, or simply Finns, are the nations who speak languages traditionally classified in the Finnic language family, and which are thought to have originated in the region of the Volga River. Currently, the largest Finnic peoples by population are the Finns, the Estonians, the Mordvins (800,000), the Mari (570,000), the Udmurts (550,000), the Komis (330,000) and the Sámi (100,000)."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/Finno-Permic_Languages0.png/330px-Finno-Permic_Languages0.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Finnic_peoples"},{"title":"Baltic Finnic peoples","blocks":[{"text":"The Baltic Finnic peoples, often simply referred to as the Finnic peoples, are the peoples inhabiting the Baltic Sea region in Northern and Eastern Europe who speak Finnic languages. They include the Finns, Estonians, Karelians, Veps, Izhorians, Votes, and Livonians. In some cases the Kvens, Ingrians, Tornedalians and speakers of Meänkieli are considered separate from the Finns."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/2-Finnic-branch.png/330px-2-Finnic-branch.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baltic_Finnic_peoples"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/Finnic_Languages_In_The_21st_Century.png/500px-Finnic_Languages_In_The_21st_Century.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail